# Accepted citation-drift candidates (issue #378).
#
# `citecheck -drift` flags a line-range citation when the prose introducing it
# shares no vocabulary with the lines it cites. That heuristic is what found the
# 74 citations that had silently moved out from under their rows — but it has a
# known false-positive class: a row often names the *property* ("no IDOR",
# "duplicate add is a 409") while the cited lines are the *implementation*, and
# the two legitimately share no words.
#
# Every line below is a candidate that was read by a human, confirmed to cite
# the right code, and accepted. Same ignore-list-with-justification shape as
# .trivyignore, .grype.yml, zap/dast.ignore and schemathesis/schemathesis.ignore:
# an entry is a written-down decision, not a mute button. A candidate that is not
# listed here FAILS the gate, and an entry here that no longer matches any
# citation ALSO fails — so this file cannot quietly accumulate dead suppressions.
#
# Format:  <doc> | <citation> | <resolved target>  # why it is a false positive
#
# The key deliberately excludes the line number *within the doc*, so editing
# prose above a citation does not churn this file. It does include the citation
# text, so changing the citation itself stops matching and correctly re-surfaces
# it for review.

